;; PA11z — the seven lifecycle stages of ONE REAL DISPATCH, measured.
;;
;; Not a source census. This is a LIVE TRACE of a dispatch that actually ran:
;; job invoke-1788708049924-13300-38749afe, claude-2 -> codex-18, role
;; library-annotator, commissioned against zaif-cascade's refusal frontier,
;; 2026-09-06T15:20:49Z -> 15:23:15Z (2m26s, 16 tool events).
;;
;; VOCABULARY (claude-1 approved 2026-09-06, forward-only). ALIGN's cells are
;; :exists / :named-only / :absent, which cannot tell "no code" apart from "a
;; person is the code". Since Joe's question is whether the stages run RATHER
;; THAN HIM DOING IT BY HAND, this census uses:
;;   :running-code  — code did it, and here is the file:line of what refuses
;;                    or records
;;   :operator-turn — it happened, but only because an agent/operator turn did
;;                    it by hand. Name the turn.
;;   :absent        — nothing did it.
;; :operator-turn is the quantity that closing-the-system-over-the-operator
;; reduces. It is the headline number of this row.
;;
;; SCOPE OF SEARCH, stated because absence claims carry it: for each stage I
;; read the job record, the live agent record, and the futon3c code on the path
;; the dispatch actually took (transport/http.clj bell path). I did NOT
;; exhaustively search every evidence store, and I did not instrument the JVM.
;; A cell marked :absent here means "not on this path and not visible in those
;; two records", not "provably nowhere".

